 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes are delivered to browsers over self-hosted WebSocket connections, providing subsecond update latency without a realtime vendor dependency. The tradeoff is owning connection capacity and reconnect reliability rather than delegating them to a provider.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+The change store maintains replayable event revisions so that a reconnecting browser can catch up from its last known revision. The delivery layer manages WebSocket sessions and pushes committed revisions to connected browsers. Browsers maintain a persistent connection with reconnect and catch-up logic to recover from transient disconnects.
 
