 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for delivery.** Committed revisions are pushed to browsers over server-managed WebSocket connections. HTTPS polling and a hosted realtime service were both considered: polling is simpler and stateless but introduces several seconds of lag and repeated read load; a hosted service reduces connection ops work but couples the board to a vendor and adds recurring per-connection cost. WebSockets provide subsecond delivery at the cost of owning connection capacity, session management, and reconnect reliability. See [`docs/decisions/delivery-approach.md`](decisions/delivery-approach.md).
